WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.870
Film Courage: We’re hoping you can give
us a brief profile of how you found distribution

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:09.170
for each of your films, from OUTSIDE SALES,
WEATHER GIRL, 6 MONTH RULE and now with CUT

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:10.170
TO THE CHASE?

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:11.170
Blayne Weaver: Sure.

00:00:11.170 --> 00:00:13.530
OUTSIDE SALES, we didn’t know what the hell
we were doing.

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:17.880
That’s a very important thing about my first
movie.

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:24.619
I was doing well acting-wise and like this
was the movie that I sold my house to finance.

00:00:24.619 --> 00:00:30.460
I had done this cartoon for Disney where I
do the voice of Peter Pan and I made a good

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:32.009
amount of money and I bought a house.

00:00:32.009 --> 00:00:36.070
And then I sold the house to finance my first
movie.

00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:42.310
We made the film and traveled all over the
film festival circuit and spent all this money.

00:00:42.310 --> 00:00:45.050
And then came out the other side and was like
“I’m broke.”

00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:49.320
I don’t have distribution for the movie
and what do I do?

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:50.690
So that one [OUTSIDE SALES] was crazy.

00:00:50.690 --> 00:00:55.700
It took about a year of just like talking
to everybody and finally we landed with this

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:58.610
company and got a DVD deal.

00:00:58.610 --> 00:01:01.780
Which again, this was when the system wasn’t
working very well.

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:04.010
So it’s like we got a DVD deal.

00:01:04.010 --> 00:01:08.749
No money up front but they threw us a big
release party and that was pretty much it.

00:01:08.749 --> 00:01:09.909
But the movie was out.

00:01:09.909 --> 00:01:11.729
That was an important thing.

00:01:11.729 --> 00:01:13.829
WEATHER GIRL was a way better experience.

00:01:13.829 --> 00:01:18.050
WEATHER GIRL got into the Slamdance film festival.

00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:19.920
We had some cool actors.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:24.100
We had Mark Harmon, Kaitlin Olson, Tricia
O’Kelley (who was on The New Adventures

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:28.619
of Old Christine) and they all came out to
Park City [Utah] with us.

00:01:28.619 --> 00:01:34.501
That helped promote the movie and got a distribution
deal right there and we sold to Lifetime [Movie

00:01:34.501 --> 00:01:40.170
Network] which was really where the money
came in so that paid back all of our investors

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:44.780
and then we had this huge international deal
where they never paid us.

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:50.969
So we had to sue this company in order to
get them to settle for 1/5th of what they

00:01:50.969 --> 00:01:51.969
owed us.

00:01:51.969 --> 00:01:54.429
Again, the system just didn’t work.

00:01:54.429 --> 00:01:59.820
It was the kind of thing where people…the
distributors…not all of them but a lot of

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:04.709
them would just refuse to pay until you sued
them because they knew you didn’t have any

00:02:04.709 --> 00:02:05.709
money.

00:02:05.709 --> 00:02:10.849
So if you sued them and got that far, then
they would settle for like 1/5th for what

00:02:10.849 --> 00:02:11.849
they owe you.

00:02:11.849 --> 00:02:17.120
Which is a lot of the…politically this kind
of business is being talked about a lot right

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:21.420
now and in my experience, it happens all the
time.

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:25.879
Like the little guy, screaming and yelling
about “Where is the money that you owe me?”

00:02:25.879 --> 00:02:27.260
and them saying “No, no, now.

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:28.260
Alright, here!

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:30.810
I’ll pay you 1/5th and you’ll be happy
to have it.”

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:33.580
Luckily that is happening less now with distributors.

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:34.870
But that was WEATHER GIRL.

00:02:34.870 --> 00:02:39.489
So with WEATHER GIRL we made all of our money
back, but before we got into profit, we had

00:02:39.489 --> 00:02:41.690
this problem with our international distributor.

00:02:41.690 --> 00:02:47.280
[With our movie] 6 MONTH RULE we premiered
at the Austin [Texas] Film Festival.

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:49.180
Film Buff came to us.

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:55.349
Film Buff is Cinetic Media’s kind of little
brother in the indie film world and they put

00:02:55.349 --> 00:02:57.239
us out on iTunes VOD and like that.

00:02:57.239 --> 00:02:58.450
It was a great experience.

00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:00.590
We’re still out there with Film Buff.

00:03:00.590 --> 00:03:02.010
6 MONTH RULE, check it out!

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:07.760
[My new movie] CUT TO THE CHASE, we finished
the film and we’ve just been traveling film

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:12.980
festival wise and like having conversations
and we met with 108 Media Corp.

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:18.150
They seem down with the movie and we certainly
speak the same language and they’re bringing

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:19.239
us out March 7th [2017]!

00:03:19.239 --> 00:03:20.239
So I’m excited!

00:03:20.239 --> 00:03:21.239
Question for the Viewers: Have you distributed
a movie?

00:03:21.239 --> 00:03:21.254
Share a story or something you’ve learned
from the process below on this Youtube video.

